136]}, "text": "Suspect three is not a story. You can go and stand next to it.\n\nAt Tie-rinz and Mycenae, Bronze Age walls rise from boulders so big you wonder who lifted them.\n\n\"Who lifted those?\" a crewman asked.\n\nAt Tie-rinz, the travel writer Paw-sayneeus finds stones so big that a pair of mules could not move even the smallest.\n\nHe reports that the Cyclopes built that wall, and that Mycenae's wall and Lion Gate are said to be theirs too.\n\nHomer: twenty-two wagons for one door. Paw-sayneeus: two mules, and the stone wins. The Greeks measured giants in wagons and mules.\n\nThe Lion Gate is about twelve fifty B.C.. People built it, and later Greeks credited the Cyclopes. Archaeologists still call the style cyclopean masonry.\n\nThe wall is real. The giants were the Greeks' explanation for it. It cannot tell us where Homer's giant began."}